{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1854 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "65ded04109499098caa2f49cd59b405c7f10384e1d5d77d5882e1abe19e635c3" :edb/db-before-t 1853 :edb/db-after-t 1854 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535166 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:29:45.880Z" 13194139535166 true] [17592186133607 1100 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/b8ece1b03ba237390f26e8d47e40013abc221edcae144ca3a9dc233438d486a3.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14057597/assets/febc66857330a11bcd7bb93952cf5b86373974f5bf87690fdcf526d4939f19ec.png) are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139535166 true] [17592186133607 1100 "Let $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2 x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nGiven that $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle$ in $V,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139535166 false] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334949 13194139535166 true] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334956 13194139535166 true] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334963 13194139535166 true] [17592186133789 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334970 13194139535166 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334921 13194139535166 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334977 13194139535166 true] [17592186133790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334984 13194139535166 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334928 13194139535166 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334935 13194139535166 true] [17592186133791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334942 13194139535166 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334991 13194139535166 true] [17592186133792 1020 #edb/ref 17592186334998 13194139535166 true] [17592186134473 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535166 true] [17592186134473 1100 "Let $V = R^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 5x_{2}y_{2} + 2x_{3}y_{3}$.\nIf $x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139535166 false] [17592186134473 1100 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139535166 true] [17592186134473 1145 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+5\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (k\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =-15-75+10k \\\\  & =-90+10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -90+10k & =0 \\\\ 10k & =90 \\\\ k & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535166 true] [17592186134477 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535166 true] [17592186134477 1100 "Let $V = C[0, 2π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 2π]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = ∫_{0}^{2π}f(t)g(t)dt$.\n\nGiven the functions $f(t) = \\cos t$ and $g(t) = \\cos (3t)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 0$\n2. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 2π$\n3. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\cos α\\cos β = \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (α - β) + \\cos (α + β))$.*" 13194139535166 false] [17592186134477 1100 "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" 13194139535166 true] [17592186134477 1145 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the given identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}{4}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )}{4}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535166 true] [17592186334921 1017 #edb/ref 17592186334922 13194139535166 true] [17592186334921 1089 "q-44093" 13194139535166 true] [17592186334921 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535166 true] [17592186334921 1100 "Let $V=C[0,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\pi ]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=1$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139535166 true] [17592186334921 1113 #uuid "7dcbda1c-c3bc-50f6-80e4-f8f54f7d6ed6" 13194139535166 true] [17592186334921 1145 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-kt){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\pi k-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi k & =0 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334923 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334924 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334925 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334926 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1000 #edb/ref 17592186334927 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1041 "selection" 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1104 #edb/ref 17592186334926 13194139535166 true] [17592186334922 1110 #uuid "b672a664-1b05-